@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+---
+title: Generic OAuth back-channel logout (Foundry patch)
+---
+
+# Generic OAuth back-channel logout
+
+This opt-in extension accepts OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0 events for
+Generic OAuth. Ordinary Grafana sessions are validated against local database
+state; they do not contact the identity provider.
+
+Configure `[auth.generic_oauth]`:
+
+```ini
+backchannel_logout = true
+oidc_issuer = https://auth.m7.rs/application/o/grafana/
+jwk_set_url = https://auth.m7.rs/application/o/grafana/jwks/
+validate_id_token = true
+use_refresh_token = false
+scopes = openid profile email grafana_roles
+```
+
+The issuer must exactly match the provider's `iss` claim, including its trailing
+slash. Both issuer and JWKS URL must be explicit HTTPS URLs. Incoming tokens
+cannot select the provider, issuer, client, keys, or verification algorithm.
+The existing `validate_id_token` setting is recommended for consistency;
+back-channel-enabled logins independently require a signed, current ID token
+with the configured issuer, client audience, subject, and issuance time.
+
+Configure Authentik's Grafana provider:
+
+```yaml
+logout_uri: https://dash.m7.rs/login/generic_oauth/backchannel-logout
+logout_method: backchannel
+```
+
+Do not replace Grafana's `signout_redirect_url`: it still starts provider logout
+when the user signs out of Grafana. `offline_access` and refresh grants are not
+needed when refresh is disabled. Local session lifetimes continue to be governed
+by `login_maximum_lifetime_duration` and `login_maximum_inactive_lifetime_duration`.
+
+## Session and event handling
+
+Login persists hashes binding the local session to the provider/client, `sub`,
+optional `sid`, and ID-token `iat`. Raw identity values are not stored in the new
+columns or logout-marker table.
+
+The public POST endpoint accepts one form-encoded `logout_token`, with a 64 KiB
+request limit. It verifies an asymmetric JWT signature, exact issuer, client
+audience, `iat`, `jti`, the logout event, optional `exp`/`nbf`, absence of `nonce`,
+and at least one of `sid`/`sub`. Issuance must be within five minutes, with ten
+seconds of clock-skew allowance. JWKS are cached for an hour and refreshed on
+verification failure, rate-limited to once per five seconds per configured URL.
+Key-fetch or database failures return 503; invalid events return 400. Only
+successful durable persistence returns 200. No browser session or CSRF cookie is
+required; the JWT authenticates the request.
+
+Events with `sid` match that provider/client session, and also `sub` if supplied.
+Events with only `sub` match all that subject's sessions for the provider/client.
+Unrelated providers, users, and local sessions remain untouched.
+
+Monotonic logout cutoffs are checked locally on every session lookup and during
+session creation. A logout racing an in-flight callback cannot resurrect usable
+credentials, and replay or out-of-order delivery cannot undo a newer logout.
+Cookie rotation does not change the original identity binding. A login whose
+ID-token issuance time is later than the event may establish a new session,
+even if the provider reuses its `sid`. Same-second login/logout is conservatively
+revoked; retry login after the next second.
+
+The implementation rejects subsequent authenticated requests; it does not close
+an already-running request, WebSocket, or rendered dashboard. Logout markers do
+not physically delete session rows, so session listings can still show them
+until normal cleanup. Revocation storage errors fail closed for bound sessions.
+
+## Rollout and limitations
+
+- Existing sessions lack verified bindings and cannot be correlated. Revoke
+  pre-rollout Grafana sessions (or require users to sign in again) before relying
+  on back-channel logout. Merely deploying the patch does not revoke them.
+- The patch is disabled by default and does not change refresh enforcement.
+  Disable refresh explicitly to obtain outage-independent sessions.
+- Delivery matters: a missed logout event cannot be inferred while Authentik is
+  unavailable. Test administrative termination and group/access removal rather
+  than assuming they emit logout events.
+- Markers deliberately have no automatic expiry. Do not truncate them while
+  older sessions or in-flight logins could still exist. A bounded cleanup policy
+  would need to account for all local session lifetimes and clock skew.
+- The migration adds nullable binding columns, a marker table and an index.
+  SQLite tests cover migrations and persistence; PostgreSQL/MySQL need separate
+  integration verification before calling this an upstream-ready feature.
+
+## Acceptance checks
+
+`scripts/test-oidc-backchannel.py /path/to/grafana /path/to/source` runs an
+isolated loopback HTTPS IdP and real Grafana server with a disposable SQLite
+database. It requires Python's `cryptography` package and checks token expiry,
+session-targeted logout, persistence across restart, and an IdP outage. These
+checks also run in Grafana's normal package check phase in Foundry.
+
+The real-provider checks still matter:
+
+1. Log in twice in separate browser sessions; confirm the expected `sid` scope.
+2. End one Authentik session and confirm only its Grafana session is rejected.
+3. Repeat after the one-minute OIDC access token has expired and after restarting
+   Grafana. Refresh must remain disabled.
+4. Stop or block Authentik; existing Grafana sessions must continue to work.
+5. Restart Authentik, log in again, and verify logout still targets the new login.
+6. Exercise subject-only events, duplicate events, key rotation, and a callback
+   racing logout. Check HTTP failure responses and the IdP's delivery behaviour.
